{"results":[{"url":"https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/16/fact-check-no-kanye-west-isnt-leading-kentucky-presidential-votes/3671444001/","final_url":"https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/16/fact-check-no-kanye-west-isnt-leading-kentucky-presidential-votes/3671444001/","title":"Fact check: Kanye West, other third-party candidates, not leading in Kentucky voting","description":"Fake election results from a local TV station's test led to confusion over who's leading in the presidential race in Kentucky.","language":"en","text":"FACT CHECK\n\nKanye West\n\n# Fact check: Kanye West, other third-party candidates, not leading in Kentucky voting\n\nElla Lee\n\nUSA TODAY\n\nOct. 16, 2020, 10:38 a.m. ET\n\n## Claim: Kentucky election results show Kanye West, Jo Jorgensen with major vote totals\n\nDonald Trump and Joe Biden aren’t the only people running for president. On some ballots, third-party candidates such a Libertarian Jo Jorgensen, independent Brock Pierce and, yes, rapper Kanye West, who’s running under his own party called the Birthday Party, will be listed below the major party candidates.\n\nBut viral posts on social media claim some of those candidates are performing stronger than is made out.\n\nThe evening of Oct. 13, West tweeted a screenshot from the website of LEX 18 News, a local Kentucky news outlet. The top of the page read “President Kentucky results: 100% of precincts reporting” which appeared to show the rapper, along with two other third-party candidates, far outperforming Biden and Trump.\n\nThe screenshot showed Jorgensen allegedly leading at the polls with 36% of the vote, followed by Pierce with 29% of the vote. West had 19% of the vote – over 40,000 votes – according to the screenshot.\n\nAfter tweeting the image, West sent another tweet – this time a video of him dancing – with the caption “GET THE WEST WING READY !!! … this is how I felt when I saw the Kentucky pole (sic) result.”\n\nMemes popped up on Facebook and Instagram shortly after showing the supposed election results, overlaid with text pointing out West’s purported victory. The people that posted the memes did not respond to USA TODAY’s requests for comment.\n\n## Test results from local TV station led to confusion\n\nThe numbers in the picture aren’t real. After West shared the image, Lex 18 News’ Twitter explained that the screenshot showed test results from a cached web link used during the state’s June primary election to post Associated Press election results.\n\n“The results shown were not valid,” Lex 18’s tweet read. “They were simply part of a test. We regret the discovery of the cached web link and have removed the data from that page.”\n\nLex 18 anchor Nancy Cox further clarified the matter on her own Twitter.\n\n\"Despite what (West) is tweeting, he is not leading the race for President in KY,\" Cox tweeted Tuesday. \"The Associated Press always sends out test numbers to make sure systems are working. It's an automated thing that is posting election results on our website. Sorry viewers. Sorry Kanye.\"\n\nTwitter later marked the images Kanye shared as “manipulated media.” A search of “President Kentucky results” on the news organization’s website turned up empty, too.\n\nWest will be on the ballot in at least 10 states this fall, according to the Louisville Courier-Journal. Jorgensen, whom the fake election results listed as leading in the polls, will be on the ballot in all 50 states and the District of Columbia, and Pierce will be on the ballot in at least 15 states.\n\n## Our rating: False\n\nThe screenshot showing a number of third-party presidential candidates leading  at the polls  in Kentucky  depicts fake election results. Kanye West has not received 40,000 votes in Kentucky. We rate this claim FALSE.\n\n## Our fact-check sources:\n\n* Kanye West's Twitter, Oct. 13\n* Kanye West's Twitter, Oct. 13\n* Lex 18 News' Twitter, Oct. 13\n* Lex 18 News Anchor Nancy Cox's Twitter, Oct. 13\n* Lex 18 News, retrieved Oct. 15, Search: \"President Kentucky results\"\n* Louisville Courier Journal, Oct. 14, 'Get the West Wing ready': Kanye West posts screenshot of invalid Kentucky polling numbers\n* Jo Jorgensen's website, Jorgensen on ballot in all 50 states plus D.C.\n* Local 22 & Local 44, Oct. 15, ‘Gordon Bombay’ of the Mighty Ducks is running for president\n\n*Thank you for supporting our journalism. You can subscribe to our print edition, ad-free app or electronic newspaper replica here.*\n\n*Our fact check work is supported in part by a grant from Facebook.*\n\n*This fact check is available at IFCN’s 2020 US Elections FactChat #Chatbot on WhatsApp. Click here, for more.*\n\nFeatured Weekly Ad","author":"Ella Lee","published_date":"2020-10-16T14:38:14Z","latency_ms":396.08762299985756,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}